{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"premium-drizzly-weaverbird","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\nTwenty-one visible intercept commands lack package-data help. `rebar help <command>` reports them unknown and handler-owned `--help` can enter execution. Current command identity is reconstructed from policy sets, help filenames, and explicit ladder arms, so help cannot be made comprehensive without another drift-prone census.\n\n## Approach\nCreate a focused stdlib-only registry module using frozen dataclasses and typed enums. Define every canonical top-level command and spelling once, including visibility, lazy handler reference, help resource, compatibility replacement metadata, mount/init/confirmation/output policy fields, and possible semantic capability names. This story uses the registry for identity/help routing and derives compatibility exports for later execution migration; it does not cut over command execution yet.\n\nValidate the registry at import without importing handlers: duplicate spellings, contradictory policy combinations, unknown capability names, exact alias ownership, retired-token collisions, and a one-to-one census between visible routes and committed help resources fail deterministically. Exact aliases are embedded in a route, so alias chains/cycles cannot form. Full handler resolution is deliberately outside normal startup and belongs to S5's full-extra contract.\n\nRoute only enough global syntax to locate a top-level spelling and reject malformed global syntax. Render top-level overview, `<command> --help`/`-h`, `help <command>`, alias help, and unknown-command output before RP-04 operation composition, config discovery/materialization, store mount, handler resolution, or capability checks. Nested help remains handler-owned.\n\nCommit reviewed package-data help for all 21 formerly intercept-only visible commands and a comprehensive grouped overview. Preserve exact bytes and stream/exit contracts. Exact aliases reuse canonical help. `bridge-fsck`, `bridge-probe`, and `jira-onboard` retain alias-specific help naming their canonical replacement; `jira-onboard` keeps its historical usage spelling. Hidden `bridge-status` resolves explicit help but remains absent from overview/discovery. `purge-bridge` remains unknown.\n\n## Compatibility and Rollback\nThis is an approved behavior change for the 21 help forms; all execution behavior remains on the current ladder. Derived compatibility exports keep existing imports and monkeypatch sites alive. Reverting this slice restores the former two-family help behavior without touching stored data.\n\n## Testing\nUse unit tests for pure registry validation. Use real CLI subprocess tests in a fresh attachable checkout with sentinels that fail on operation composition, config reads, mount, handler resolution, and optional imports. Assert exact committed bytes, stdout/stderr, exit codes, all alias classes, grouped overview uniqueness, malformed global syntax, unknown commands, nested help lean behavior, and a negative execution control that still crosses operation/mount boundaries.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every canonical visible command has exactly one committed help resource and appears exactly once in the grouped overview; no alias is duplicated as a canonical entry.\n- [ ] Both top-level help forms return byte-identical committed help without composing config, mounting a store, resolving a handler, or importing an optional dependency.\n- [ ] Exact, compatibility, and hidden aliases exhibit the approved help/discovery rules; `jira-onboard` retains its historical usage and `purge-bridge` remains unknown.\n- [ ] Registry validation rejects every synthetic duplicate spelling, policy contradiction, unknown capability, stray/missing help resource, and retired-token collision without importing a handler.\n- [ ] Existing command execution remains on the compatibility path, and a real execution negative control still reaches RP-04 composition and the established mount/init boundary.","id":"2635-4aa3-5c14-4b12","parent_id":"09ae-e827-1cac-452d","priority":1,"tags":["rp05","cli"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"RP-05 S2 — Serve canonical top-level help from the lazy route registry"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787069570378383001,"uuid":"1651698f-3c8c-4b4f-bb0e-0cbdd3333a84"}